@* The wall update wizard's key bindings (including the review steppers), lifted out of
   KeyboardShortcuts.razor so that page stays under the project's file-size rule. Static markup only —
   content is identical to what the page rendered before. *@

    <section class="keyshort-section">
        <h2>Wall update wizard</h2>
        <p class="keyshort-note">The wizard declares a different set of keys on each step.</p>
        <dl class="keyshort-list">
            <div class="keyshort-row">
                <dt><kbd>Enter</kbd></dt>
                <dd>
                    The primary button of the current step: <em>Resume update</em> on the resume prompt,
                    <em>Start update</em> on the upload step, <em>Continue</em> on the detected-holds step,
                    <em>Continue to overlaps</em> on the carry-over step, <em>Continue</em> on the touch-up
                    step, <em>Apply update</em> on the confirm step, and <em>Done</em> on the final step.
                    It respects the button's own disabled state, so a premature <kbd>Enter</kbd> does
                    nothing. If you have tabbed or clicked onto some other button, that button keeps
                    <kbd>Enter</kbd> and is what gets pressed.
                </dd>
            </div>
            <div class="keyshort-row">
                <dt><kbd>s</kbd></dt>
                <dd>
                    Skip touch-up &mdash; on the detected-holds and touch-up steps only. On the
                    detected-holds step skipping and continuing are the same action.
                </dd>
            </div>
            <div class="keyshort-row">
                <dt><kbd>a</kbd></dt>
                <dd>
                    The <em>add hold</em> tool &mdash; on the detected-holds, touch-up and carry-over steps.
                    Tap the photo to place a hold at the toolbar's current size. Press <kbd>a</kbd> again
                    to leave the tool.
                </dd>
            </div>
            <div class="keyshort-row">
                <dt><kbd>m</kbd></dt>
                <dd>
                    The <em>select / move</em> tool &mdash; same three steps, and the same letter the wall
                    editor uses. Holds can only be dragged while it is active: outside it a drag on the
                    photo scrolls and zooms as usual. Press <kbd>m</kbd> again to leave the tool.
                </dd>
            </div>
            <div class="keyshort-row">
                <dt><kbd>d</kbd></dt>
                <dd>
                    The <em>delete hold</em> tool &mdash; same three steps. Tap a hold to delete it; press
                    <kbd>d</kbd> again to leave the tool.
                </dd>
            </div>
            <div class="keyshort-row">
                <dt><kbd>p</kbd></dt>
                <dd>
                    The <em>pipette</em> tool &mdash; same three steps. Tap a hold to copy its size onto the
                    holds you add next; you land back in the add tool.
                </dd>
            </div>
            <div class="keyshort-row">
                <dt><kbd>x</kbd></dt>
                <dd>Remove the selected hold &mdash; same three steps; does nothing when nothing is selected</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="keyshort-row">
                <dt><kbd>Esc</kbd></dt>
                <dd>
                    Close the flow &mdash; only on the resume prompt (<em>Not now</em>), the upload step
                    (<em>Cancel</em>) and the final step (<em>Done</em>)
                </dd>
            </div>
        </dl>
        <p class="keyshort-note">
            <kbd>Esc</kbd> never discards a staged update. It is bound only where closing throws nothing
            away, and is deliberately unbound on the detected-holds, carry-over, neighbour-overlap,
            touch-up and confirm steps, because discarding is irreversible and would go unconfirmed.
            The neighbour-overlap step declares no wizard keys at all &mdash; it is driven entirely by the
            overlap stepper below. The carry-over step also goes quiet while one of its review sub-views
            is open.
        </p>

        <h3>Review steppers</h3>
        <p class="keyshort-note">
            Used while reviewing matched or carried-over holds. While a stepper is on screen it owns the
            keyboard: every shortcut above &mdash; the wizard's own keys and the wall carousel arrows
            included &mdash; is inactive, and only the keys below apply.
        </p>
        <p class="keyshort-note">
            They are the stepper's own keys, so they go to whatever has focus. The stepper takes focus
            itself when it opens and again after every decision, so you can normally just start typing;
            if you clicked something else in between, click the stepper once to hand focus back. Nothing
            typed into the hold-size slider reaches them, so its own <kbd>&larr;</kbd>/<kbd>&rarr;</kbd>
            never also steps the review.
        </p>
        <dl class="keyshort-list">
            <div class="keyshort-row">
                <dt><kbd>Enter</kbd></dt>
                <dd>Confirm the match / accept the carried hold (keep, for new holds)</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="keyshort-row">
                <dt><kbd>c</kbd></dt>
                <dd>Confirm match (neighbour overlaps) / toggle &ldquo;changed&rdquo; (carry-over, except the new-hold list)</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="keyshort-row">
                <dt><kbd>d</kbd></dt>
                <dd>
                    Discard match &mdash; neighbour-overlap stepper only. On the carry-over stepper and the
                    side-by-side confirm the same letter is the <em>delete hold</em> tool instead (see below);
                    the two steppers never show both meanings at once.
                </dd>
            </div>
            <div class="keyshort-row">
                <dt><kbd>m</kbd></dt>
                <dd>
                    Mark hold as moved &mdash; neighbour-overlap stepper only. Unrelated to the wizard's
                    own <kbd>m</kbd>: while a stepper is on screen it owns the keyboard.
                </dd>
            </div>
            <div class="keyshort-row">
                <dt><kbd>x</kbd> <span class="keyshort-plus">or</span> <kbd>Del</kbd></dt>
                <dd>
                    Remove the hold / discard the new hold. <kbd>Del</kbd> works here only &mdash; outside
                    a stepper it is not bound at all, and the wizard's own remove key is <kbd>x</kbd>.
                </dd>
            </div>
            <div class="keyshort-row">
                <dt><kbd>&larr;</kbd> <kbd>&rarr;</kbd></dt>
                <dd>Previous / next item</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="keyshort-row">
                <dt><kbd>Esc</kbd></dt>
                <dd>
                    Cancel the current sub-mode (manual pairing, &ldquo;moved&rdquo; pick, interactive pick),
                    or leave the hold touch-up tool where one is active. It never discards anything.
                </dd>
            </div>
        </dl>
        <p class="keyshort-note">
            While a sub-mode is open the stepper only listens for <kbd>Enter</kbd> (confirm the pick, and
            <kbd>c</kbd> does the same in the &ldquo;moved&rdquo; pick) and <kbd>Esc</kbd> (cancel it).
        </p>

        <h3>Hold touch-up tools inside a review</h3>
        <p class="keyshort-note">
            The carry-over stepper, the side-by-side confirm and the neighbour-overlap stepper all carry
            the same hold toolbar as the touch-up step, so a wrong hold can be added, moved, resized or
            deleted without backing out to the overview. It only ever edits the <strong>new</strong>
            (right-hand) photo. Picking a tool leaves the surface's own tap mode &mdash; the re-target
            pick, the link pick, the &ldquo;moved&rdquo; pick &mdash; and leaving the tool gives it back,
            so a tap never means two things at once.
        </p>
        <dl class="keyshort-list">
            <div class="keyshort-row">
                <dt><kbd>a</kbd></dt>
                <dd>The <em>add hold</em> tool &mdash; carry-over stepper and side-by-side confirm</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="keyshort-row">
                <dt><kbd>m</kbd></dt>
                <dd>The <em>select / move</em> tool &mdash; carry-over stepper and side-by-side confirm</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="keyshort-row">
                <dt><kbd>d</kbd></dt>
                <dd>The <em>delete hold</em> tool &mdash; carry-over stepper and side-by-side confirm</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="keyshort-row">
                <dt><kbd>p</kbd></dt>
                <dd>The <em>pipette</em> tool &mdash; carry-over stepper and side-by-side confirm</dd>
            </div>
        </dl>
        <p class="keyshort-note">
            <strong>The neighbour-overlap stepper has no tool keys</strong> &mdash; its toolbar is
            buttons only. <kbd>c</kbd>, <kbd>d</kbd>, <kbd>m</kbd> and <kbd>x</kbd> are already its
            confirm / discard / moved / delete review actions, and those were not re-lettered for the
            tools. The side-by-side confirm had no keys of its own at all before these four, and the
            carry-over stepper's own keys (<kbd>Enter</kbd>, <kbd>c</kbd>, <kbd>x</kbd>, the arrows)
            leave <kbd>a</kbd>/<kbd>m</kbd>/<kbd>d</kbd>/<kbd>p</kbd> free.
        </p>
        <p class="keyshort-note">
            The tools also appear on the neighbour-overlap stepper only during a <strong>wall
            update</strong>. In the add-a-panel flow that stepper has no staged update behind it, so the
            toolbar is not shown there at all.
        </p>
    </section>
